From 5d14407ca782e9ecc91ec9977b1e326208a85616 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:33:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GBM: HDR10_ADAPT, whether an HDR10 or HDR10+ video is adapted
 to the display

What decides, without GL, so that a host test runs it: for the setting
of the next commit (never, always, automatic), what the first picture of
a video says of itself (PQ, BT.2020 with its matrix, progressive, 2D,
not Dolby Vision; its MaxCLL and the peak of its mastering display) and
what the display is (an output that takes HDR10, its peak).

Automatic adapts only a video whose peak is above that of the display.
The peak of the video for that is its MaxCLL where the stream gives one
that can be used, else the peak of its mastering display: MaxCLL is the
brightest pixel of the content, the mastering display only what it could
show (a film mastered on a monitor of 4000 cd/m2 may have nothing above
1000, and on a display of 1000 it is left as it is). The curve itself
takes the mastering display first and MaxCLL only where that is missing
(libplacebo, pl_color_space_nominal_luma_ex()), so it never maps from a
MaxCLL that is given too low. Without either peak, or without a peak of
the display, nothing is mapped (the curve would have to guess). Never a
Dolby Vision picture (its own, or HDR10 sent as Dolby Vision to a display
that takes it natively), nor an output that takes no HDR10 (an SDR output
maps HDR10 to SDR as before). The 99.98th percentile that Hades measures
exists only where native Dolby Vision is drawn: not used here.
---
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDR10Adapt.h | 176 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 176 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDR10Adapt.h

diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDR10Adapt.h b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDR10Adapt.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..871b424
--- /dev/null
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HDR10Adapt.h
@@ -0,0 +1,176 @@
+/*
